\section*{Least volume of nongeodesic minimal hypersurfaces in spheres}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
\textit{Least volume of nongeodesic minimal hypersurfaces}: The announced lower bound for a non-totally-geodesic closed minimal immersion into a round sphere is the volume of the smallest Clifford product in the same dimension. Domain volume counts any covering multiplicity.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
\textit{Least volume of nongeodesic minimal hypersurfaces}: A family based on Lorentz-distance parameters detects the relevant geometric class cohomologically and feeds a min--max comparison. Average-curvature and index rigidity control the extremizing configuration, while low-density cone analysis supplies attainment. The immersion reduction preserves multiplicity, so the final volume comparison concerns the domain rather than only its image.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
\textit{Least volume of nongeodesic minimal hypersurfaces}: The announced lower bound for a non-totally-geodesic closed minimal immersion into a round sphere is the volume of the smallest Clifford product in the same dimension. Domain volume counts any covering multiplicity. The parameters, regularity assumptions and normalization in this question are retained in applying that assertion.

With the hypotheses and conventions above, the claimed result addresses the whole mathematical assertion on this page.

\subsection{Formal verification}
\textit{Least volume of nongeodesic minimal hypersurfaces}: The pinned catalogue lists no Lean formalization for this result.

This site has not run the Lean development. These links describe OpenAI’s selected formal statements and should be read with their stated scope.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI reports that an internal model produced the manuscripts. Their arguments have not been independently verified as proofs by this site. Any portion outside the documented formal scope may contain mistakes, and the catalogue label reports the announced mathematical claim.

